  "released": "2026-09-24",
  "changelog": "v345: a telling of a landmark folds onto it, and a landmark is drawn as what it is. WHERE IT WAS SEEN: the owner's own review of staging on 2026-09-24, reproduced on a scratch clone of his vault at `5bc68bd9` with v342 bytes. Three cards asked when things happened that the vault already dates, and one node was a birth nobody has ever had. `node:058637e49ae30122189fc6c8` \"Mom married dad at 21\" carried ONE `age` claim (`claim:34c92ccbf218e122a181ca5f`, age 21, `subject_mention: mother`, from `answers/K14.md` — \"Mom got married young, at age 21\") and no date at all, while `node:6ece5329bd87d55477f71a54` \"Parents' wedding date\" sat beside it at 1976-06-25 stated, from `answers/Q4.md`. `node:b99fbe3d9b3c9b999744df68` \"Harvey arriving\" carried one `occurrence` claim whose own evidence reads \"Birth of Harvey during the Etherfuse chapter\", and Harvey's birth had been dated 2021-10-11 for weeks on a ten-telling episode. And `node:9ca9a5b15cd988e30132eb98` was drawn \"Parents's birth\" at that same 1976-06-25: `state/landmarks.json` holds exactly one `family` entry, `{\"label\":\"parents\",\"who\":\"parents\",\"relation\":\"parent\",\"date\":\"1976-06-25\"}` — his parents' WEDDING — and `family` declares `date_semantics: birth`, so the drawing read a couple's anniversary as a birthday. WHAT BROKE: three separate places where the substrate could not see what it already held. (1) A landmark entry's date was drawn as the event its DOMAIN declares, and `family` is the one domain whose entries are not all the same shape — a sibling entry dates a sibling's birth and a couple entry dates a wedding, and a couple is never born. (2) The milestone rung `R2b` keys on (milestone, one person) and reads the milestone from the `event_kind` or the label's own VERB — so \"arriving\" was not a birth (\"Birth of\" was in the evidence, where nothing looked), \"wedding\" was not a marriage (no verb in \"Parents' wedding date\"), and a wedding's two people were two keys rather than one couple (\"Mom married dad at 21\" names `mother`; \"Parents' wedding date\" names `parents`; `_same_people` refuses them). (3) An `age` claim was measured against the birth of the GROUP's subject, so an age about the owner's mother on a node about his parents had no anchor at all — and when it does have one, an age window beside a stated day was going to be published as a rival reading rather than as the agreeing evidence it is. THE RULES, three, each with one statement of itself and one seat. `landmark_projection.A_LANDMARK_IS_DRAWN_AS_WHAT_IT_IS`: `entry_date_event_kind` is the one definition of what an ENTRY's date dates, refining a declared `birth` exactly three ways — an entry that STATES a birth stays one, an entry whose own words name a marriage or whose subject is exactly ONE couple is `married`, and a collective that is not one couple — `grandparents`, because a person has up to four of them and two of their weddings, or any enumeration of two or more names — is the existing `transition`, \"a date whose event the record does not say\". `COUPLE_SUBJECT_WORDS` is compared WHOLE and casefolded exactly as `is_birth_domain_word` is, so \"Parents\" is a couple and \"Parenteau\" is a man. Read at THREE seats — `entry_claims` at filing, `_attach_dates` and the fold's `read_landmark_dates` at draw — which is what makes a vault that already holds the record heal on its next redraw with no migration, the way v339's item 10 and v341's amendment do; the receipts on disk still say what the importer read out of the record, and the drawing says what the record means. `episode_binder.A_TELLING_OF_A_LANDMARK_FOLDS_ONTO_IT`: `milestone_of` reads the label's own NOUN (`MILESTONE_OF_EVENT_NOUN` — `wedding` alone, because `marriage` is a STATE a life spends years inside and the owner's vault holds nine nodes that prove it, and `anniversary` recurs; a noun after a preposition is an adjunct, so \"Father fell ill after wedding\" is an illness) and then the EVIDENCE, through `landmark_projection.names_a_birth` — the three shapes a birth is said in (`birth of X`, `X's birth`, `X arriving`/`X was born`) over `BIRTH_PHRASE_WORDS`, derived from `BIRTH_DOMAIN_WORDS` rather than re-listed, plus one closed arrival-verb list. The name the sentence gives must be somebody the telling is ALREADY about, because \"the birth of the company\" is not a person's birth and no word list can tell the difference; the classifier's own when-metadata is cut before the shapes are read (`BIRTH_PHRASE_WHEN_MARKERS`) and a birth introduced by a temporal preposition is an adjunct (`BIRTH_PHRASE_IS_AN_ADJUNCT_AFTER`) — \"shortly after James's birth\" dates a flight, and the first run of this release folded that flight (2013-06/2013-07) onto a birthday (2013-05-10) before the guard existed. And a marriage is once per COUPLE: `identity_resolution.ONCE_PER_COUPLE_EVENT_KINDS` and `couple_key`, swept as a second bucket beside the per-person ones, where `_same_people` is not asked because the bucket key has already asserted the stronger thing. `grandparents` is deliberately absent from `COUPLE_OF_RELATION_WORD` for the reason `graduation` is absent from `ONCE_PER_SUBJECT_EVENT_KINDS` — a person has up to four grandparents and two of their weddings, and the bare word discriminates neither. `temporal_timeline.AN_AGE_AND_A_DATE_CORROBORATE`, the owner's ruling verbatim (\"there are two ways for you to have resolved this\"): an age is the age of the person the CLAIM is about, not the node's subject, and an age window that CONTAINS the date a node is placed at joins that placement's provenance as agreeing evidence and is never published as a rival — while one that EXCLUDES it stays a rival, so `chronology.conflict_strength` scores the disagreement and the contradiction card names both dates. `age_corroborates_placement` and `age_contradicts_placement` say which happened out loud. The arithmetic is the package's own and there is exactly one of it: `chronology.from_age_band` is already day-exact for a day-precise birth, so Desiree's 1955-06-19 plus 21 is 1976-06-19/1977-06-18 and the wedding's 1976-06-25 is inside it. THE FOURTH WAY A NODE ID MOVES, and the one neither a bind nor a mint can see: a node id is derived FROM its event kind, so an entry read as a wedding today is drawn at a different id than the birth it was drawn at yesterday while every work item, session and URL still names the old one. v342 closed three ways and this is the fourth — `temporal_timeline._landmark_redraw_aliases` publishes the redirect under `episode_fold.AN_ALIAS_NEVER_NAMES_A_NODE_THE_DRAWING_PUBLISHES` and with the carry's own two refusals (a key some claim still mints is not redirected, and a claim carrying its own `event_ref` never moved), reports `landmark_date_kind_redrawn` for every row, and loses to the identity layer's tables on a collision, because a person's decision outranks a redraw. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` takes `timeline-rules:14`: the same claims calculate to a different node set, a different alternates set and a different work-item set. RE-RUN on a fresh clone of the owner's vault at `5bc68bd9` with these bytes — publish (redraw), `bind-episodes --apply` (37 envelopes, 199 proposals, 223 record groups), publish. Generation 151 -> 152 -> 153; nodes 1189 -> 1189 -> 1185, placed 1153 -> 1153 -> 1151, `node_aliases` 966 -> 967 -> 997, open cards 45 -> 45 -> 43 (`precision_gap` 35 -> 35 -> 33, `contradiction` 7 -> 7 -> 7, `identity_uncertain` 3 -> 3 -> 3). All three of the owner's complaints are gone and the REDRAW ALONE fixes the third: \"Parents's birth\" is not drawn, `node:9ca9a5b1…` redirects to a `married` node labelled \"Parents' wedding\" at 1976-06-25, and no vault write happened. After the apply, \"Mom married dad at 21\" and \"Parents' wedding date\" are ONE node dated 1976-06-25 with no card on it — the age telling bound rather than contradicted, because the vault already holds Desiree's 1955-06-19 and 1976-06-25 is inside the window it implies — and \"Harvey arriving\" is inside Harvey's birth episode at 2021-10-11 with no card either. The AUDIT is empty at both steps: 0 placements lost, 0 dated moments moved, 0 nodes drawn at an id `node_aliases` redirects, measured over every node of the vault rather than over named ones. Two `married` nodes remain at 1976-06-25 — the folded pair and the landmark record's own. The landmark node IS a legitimate R2 target now and both links to it are minted (`R2b married:parents` to \"Parents' wedding date\", `R2b married:couple:parents` to \"Mom married dad at 21\"), but `exact_identity_groups` grows its cliques GREEDILY over the tellings in sorted order and a telling already taken by an earlier clique is not revisited — so by the time the sweep reaches a `landmark:` ref, which sorts after every `classification:` one, both of its neighbours are spoken for and it forms no clique of its own. That is v333's own non-transitivity doctrine working as written (the clique rule is what cut a 104-telling transitive closure back to the births it was about), and changing the sweep's order is a release of its own rather than a line in this one. What matters for the owner's page is done: neither node carries a card, and the birth nobody has ever had is gone from both. REGRESSIONS CAUGHT AND GUARDED IN THE SAME PASS: run 1 of this release folded a FLIGHT (\"Katie and James fly to Seattle\", 2013-06/2013-07) onto a birthday (2013-05-10) because \"shortly after James's birth\" read as a birth; run 2 read a four-year-old's declaration about her brother the same way. Both are fixtures now, and the audit that found them is the test that keeps them found. Guards: `tests/test_v345_a_telling_of_a_landmark_folds_onto_it.py`, 57 tests — the three owner shapes as fixtures on a synthetic vault (the `family` couple entry filed as the PRE-v345 filer wrote it, so \"heals on redraw\" is tested against the receipts the vault actually holds), the wedding noun and the nine state-word nodes it must not read, the Seattle flight the adjunct guard exists for, the couple key across two vocabularies, both halves of the corroboration ruling, and v340's promise restated as an INVARIANT over whole vaults with its own arithmetic tested against a loss, a move, a ghost and an alias chain. Docs: the I0 fold contract gains its v345 amendment, the landmarks handbook two rows, the eras handbook the rule-version row. Deliberately NOT built here: the binder's greedy clique sweep, which is why the landmark's own wedding node and the folded pair's are still two nodes at one date — the links between them are minted and refused by nothing; `exact_identity_groups` simply reaches the `landmark:` ref after its neighbours are taken. Also `entry_supported_event_kinds` still answers per DOMAIN, so a `family` couple entry's wedding is `contextual_only` on the owner's axis exactly as it was before — widening owner relevance is an axis-membership decision, not a drawing one; and the wedding node of a couple still mints its relationship edge against the OWNER as counterpart, which is every `married` node's existing shape and not this release's to change.",
